USE health_ai_platform;

-- ============================================================
-- Real policy content for the 5 core Adjudication knowledge_docs
-- (v1/v2/v3 of each = 15 rows total), replacing the Faker
-- gibberish generated by generate_seed_data.py.
-- ============================================================

-- --- Adjudication Rule Engine Overview -----------------------------
UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Every claim passes through the adjudication rule engine in a fixed order: Duplicate Check first (does this claim match an already-processed claim on member, provider, date of service, and CPT code), then Timely Filing (was the claim submitted within the filing window), then COB Rule (is another payer primary), then Bundling Edit (should this code be bundled into another billed code on the same claim), then Medical Necessity (does the diagnosis/procedure combination meet coverage criteria), and finally Fee Schedule Cap (does the billed amount exceed the contracted rate). A claim failing any rule is either DENY (hard stop, e.g. duplicate or timely filing failure), ADJUST (the claim proceeds but the paid amount is modified, e.g. fee schedule cap), or PEND (held for manual adjudicator review, e.g. an ambiguous medical necessity case). Rules after a DENY are not evaluated — adjudication stops at the first hard-stop failure.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Adjudication Rule Engine Overview';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 2 clarifies the rule ordering in v1 with an explicit exception: COB Rule is evaluated before Duplicate Check, not after, when the claim itself indicates a coordination-of-benefits scenario (a secondary-payer EOB is attached), since the duplicate check logic needs to know whether this is legitimately a secondary submission of a claim already paid by the primary payer, not a true duplicate. This revision also adds a rule-skip audit log requirement: any time a rule is skipped due to an earlier hard-stop DENY, the skip must be recorded, not just the final decision, so a later re-adjudication (per the Pended Claims Review SOP) has full visibility into what was and wasn''t evaluated.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Adjudication Rule Engine Overview v2';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 3 adds a rule-version pinning requirement: a claim''s adjudication must use the rule engine version in effect on the date of service, not the date of adjudication, since fee schedules and bundling edits change over time (see Fee Schedule Cap Policy) and a claim adjudicated months after service should still be evaluated against the rules that applied when the service was rendered. This revision also formalizes that a PEND decision from v1 is not itself final — every PEND must be re-evaluated within 5 business days per the Pended Claims Review SOP, and a claim cannot remain in PEND status indefinitely.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Adjudication Rule Engine Overview v3';

-- --- Bundling Edit Reference -----------------------------
UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Bundling edits prevent separate payment for procedure codes that are considered part of a more comprehensive code billed on the same claim (e.g. a surgical approach code bundled into the primary surgery code). The bundling edit table is sourced from CMS National Correct Coding Initiative (NCCI) edits, refreshed quarterly. When a bundling edit applies, the component code is denied with an ADJUST decision (not a hard DENY) and the adjustment_amount reflects the value of the bundled-out code — the claim as a whole is not rejected, only the specific line item. Modifier 59 (or an appropriate X-modifier) may unbundle an edit when clinically appropriate and properly documented; adjudicators must verify modifier usage against the clinical notes before honoring an unbundling request, not accept the modifier at face value.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Bundling Edit Reference';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 2 adds a provider-notification requirement to the ADJUST-not-DENY handling in v1: every bundling edit application must be reflected in the claim''s EOB with the specific NCCI edit pair cited, not just "bundled per policy," since providers frequently appeal bundling edits without understanding which specific code pair triggered it. This revision also tightens the modifier-verification standard in v1: a modifier 59/X-modifier unbundling request now requires the clinical notes to explicitly document a distinct procedural service, not merely be silent on the question — silence is treated as insufficient support, not as implicit justification.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Bundling Edit Reference v2';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 3 adds a same-provider, same-day scope clarification: the bundling edit table in v1 and v2 applies across all claims from the same provider for the same member on the same date of service, even if billed on separate claim submissions, not just within a single claim — a common workaround was splitting a bundled service across two claim submissions to bypass the edit. This revision also adds a quarterly refresh audit: any code pair added or removed in the NCCI update must be validated against a sample of recently adjudicated claims before the new edit table goes live, to catch mapping errors before they affect claim volume.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Bundling Edit Reference v3';

-- --- COB Adjudication Order -----------------------------
UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'When adjudicating a claim for a member with coordination of benefits on file, this plan processes as secondary using the primary payer''s EOB: the allowed amount is the lesser of this plan''s own allowed amount or the primary payer''s allowed amount, and this plan''s payment is calculated as its allowed amount minus the primary payer''s payment, never exceeding what this plan would have paid as primary. Claims indicating COB but missing the required primary payer EOB are set to PEND, not DENY, with a request sent to the provider for the missing EOB. This mirrors the COB Determination Guide used by the Claims department for eligibility determination — this document covers the adjudication calculation itself, not who is determined primary.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'COB Adjudication Order';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 2 adds explicit handling for a primary-payer denial: when the attached EOB shows the primary payer denied the claim (rather than paying a reduced amount), this plan adjudicates as if primary, using its own standard rules from the Adjudication Rule Engine Overview, rather than mechanically applying the secondary-payer formula in v1 to a zero primary payment. This revision also clarifies that a Medicare EOB showing a contractual write-off (not a "payment") is still treated as the primary payer''s allowed amount for the v1 calculation, since Medicare''s allowed amount — not its actual payment — is the relevant figure for the COB formula.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'COB Adjudication Order v2';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 3 adds a COB recalculation trigger: if a primary payer retroactively adjusts or reprocesses a claim after this plan has already paid as secondary, the secondary payment must be recalculated using the updated primary EOB, and any overpayment identified follows the standard provider or member refund process rather than being written off automatically. This revision also formalizes the PEND-not-DENY handling from v1 for missing EOBs with an explicit SLA: a COB-pended claim must be re-reviewed within 10 business days of receiving the missing documentation, matching the general Pended Claims Review SOP timeline.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'COB Adjudication Order v3';

-- --- Fee Schedule Cap Policy -----------------------------
UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'When a provider''s billed amount exceeds the contracted fee schedule rate for a CPT/HCPCS code, the claim is not denied — it is adjudicated with an ADJUST decision, and the allowed amount is capped at the contracted rate, with the adjustment_amount reflecting the difference between billed and allowed. Out-of-network claims use a different cap: the lesser of billed charges or a percentage of the Medicare fee schedule (specified in the member''s plan document), since there is no contracted rate for an out-of-network provider. Fee schedule caps apply per line item, not per claim as a whole — a claim with multiple procedure codes may have some lines capped and others paid at the full billed amount.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Fee Schedule Cap Policy';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 2 adds an annual fee schedule effective-date rule matching the Contract Renewal Checklist used by Facility & Providers: a claim''s cap calculation in v1 must use the fee schedule rate in effect on the date of service, not the date of adjudication, consistent with the rule-version pinning principle in the Adjudication Rule Engine Overview v3. This revision also adds a provider dispute path: a provider disputing a specific fee schedule rate assignment may request a rate review, which does not pause adjudication of the current claim at the current rate while under review — the claim adjudicates normally and any correction is applied retroactively if the review changes the rate.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Fee Schedule Cap Policy v2';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 3 adds a value-based rate override: providers meeting quality benchmarks per their contract (referenced in Facility & Providers'' Fee Schedule Update Policy) may have a provider-specific rate that supersedes the standard fee schedule cap in v1 and v2 for their claims specifically — the adjudication engine must check for a provider-specific override before applying the standard schedule. This revision also clarifies the out-of-network percentage-of-Medicare cap from v1 is recalculated annually alongside the standard fee schedule update, rather than remaining fixed at a stale Medicare rate reference.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Fee Schedule Cap Policy v3';

-- --- Pended Claims Review SOP -----------------------------
UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'A claim in PEND status must be reviewed by an adjudicator within 5 business days of being pended — PEND is a temporary holding status pending additional information or manual review, not a final decision. Common pend reasons include: missing COB documentation, an ambiguous medical necessity determination requiring clinical review, or a duplicate-check match requiring manual confirmation of true-duplicate versus legitimate separate encounter. A pended claim aging past 10 business days without resolution is automatically escalated to the adjudication supervisor. Once reviewed, a pended claim receives a final decision (APPROVE, DENY, or ADJUST) — it should never remain in PEND as a permanent outcome.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Pended Claims Review SOP';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 2 shortens the standard review window in v1 from 5 to 3 business days for claims pended due to a duplicate-check match specifically, since these typically require only a quick manual lookup rather than clinical judgment, and the 5-day window was found unnecessarily slow for this pend reason. This revision also adds a documentation requirement matching the standard used across this platform: the review must record which specific factor resolved the pend (the missing EOB was received, the clinical review confirmed necessity, etc.), not just the final decision.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Pended Claims Review SOP v2';

UPDATE knowledge_docs SET content = 'Revision 3 adds a provider-notification requirement: a claim pended for missing documentation (COB EOB, clinical records) must trigger an outbound request to the provider within 1 business day of the pend, rather than the provider only discovering the pend status when they check claim status directly — the original v1 process was found to leave providers unaware they needed to submit anything. This revision also formalizes the escalation threshold from v1: the 10-business-day auto-escalation applies per pend instance, meaning a claim re-pended after an unsuccessful first review restarts its own 10-day clock rather than inheriting time already elapsed from the first pend.'
WHERE dept_id = 7 AND title = 'Pended Claims Review SOP v3';
